# Conventions — TypeScript style in app/

## Context

`app/tsconfig.json` enables `strict` and `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, and every
existing module (`store.ts`, `reducer.ts`, `actions.ts`, `selectors.ts`,
`lib/text.ts`) uses named exports and immutable data. New code must match, or
`npm run typecheck` and the reducer's change-detection guarantees break.

## Rule

- Named exports ONLY. NEVER write `export default` — every module in
  `app/src/` exports named functions and types.
- NO `any`, NO `@ts-ignore`, NO `@ts-expect-error` to silence errors. The
  project compiles clean under `strict` + `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` — fix the
  type instead of suppressing it.
- State updates in `app/src/reducer.ts` are immutable: build new objects and
  arrays with spread, `map`, and `filter`, exactly like the existing
  `"task/toggled"` and `"task/removed"` cases.
- Filenames are kebab-case/lowercase (a new helper module is `date-utils.ts`,
  never `dateUtils.ts`); types and interfaces are PascalCase (`AppState`, `Task`).
- Use `import type { ... }` for type-only imports, as `app/src/reducer.ts` and
  `app/src/actions.ts` already do.

## How to verify

1. `grep -rn "export default" app/src` → zero matches.
2. `grep -rnE ":\s*any\b|@ts-ignore|@ts-expect-error" app/src` → zero matches.
3. `ls app/src app/src/lib` → every filename is kebab-case/lowercase.
4. `cd app && npm run typecheck` → exits 0 with no errors.
